<h3 id="random-collection-elements">Random Collection Elements</h3>

<p>First, you can think of <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swift/collection"><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Collection</code></a> as a type that implements the collection protocol, like a class inheriting from <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">NSObject</code>. E.g., arrays, dictionaries, etc.</p>

<p>These <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Collection</code> types all have a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">randomElement()</code> method (you can refer to the array of 10 strings introduced at the end of the previous article). This function returns an <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Optional</code> type, because the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Collection</code> might be empty.</p>

<div class="language-swift highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><!-- <td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
2
3
</pre></td> --><td class="rouge-code"><pre><span class="k">let</span> <span class="nv">emptyRange</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mi">10</span><span class="o">..&lt;</span><span class="mi">10</span>
<span class="n">emptyRange</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">isEmpty</span> <span class="c1">// → true</span>
<span class="n">emptyRange</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="nf">randomElement</span><span class="p">()</span> <span class="c1">// → nil</span>
</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div></div>
<blockquote>
  <p>As you can see, the random element is nil</p>
</blockquote>

